{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: one threat-model question, and nothing gets implemented until you answer it. Planner sequenced this row behind two other lanes, so there is no hurry — a decline closes it just as cleanly as a confirmation.\n\nTHE QUESTION. Is the operator's own process environment inside gitseq's threat model for ambient-injection defence, or is it trusted?\n\nWHAT PROMPTED IT. internal/gitstore is the boundary the kernel reads objects through. It already defends against ambient injection in one direction: hermeticGitEnvironment strips GIT_CONFIG and GIT_CONFIG_*, and pins the system and global config files to /dev/null, and every command it runs pins --no-replace-objects --git-dir. So somebody decided that a variable in the environment must not be able to change what the kernel reads.\n\nThat defence covers the configuration family and not the routing family. I measured the gap with two repositories:\n\n  git --no-replace-objects --git-dir \u003cvictim\u003e/.git cat-file -p \u003cobject\u003e\n\nwhere the object exists only in the attacker repository. Clean, it fails: \"Not a valid object name\". With GIT_COMMON_DIR pointing at the attacker, it prints the attacker's object. The --git-dir pin does not stop it. Refs did not redirect in the same test — rev-parse of a ref present in both returned the victim's — so what I have shown is object reads, not ref resolution.\n\nWHY IT IS A QUESTION AND NOT A BUG REPORT. Anyone who can set your environment variables already has a great deal of control over your machine, and it is entirely reasonable for a tool to treat the operator's own shell as trusted. Plenty of good software does. If that is the position, this row closes and the asymmetry is deliberate.\n\nBut hermeticGitEnvironment exists, which says the opposite about the configuration family, and I cannot tell from the code whether leaving the routing family out was reasoned or simply not considered. The two families do the same kind of thing. That asymmetry is the whole of my doubt.\n\nWHAT CHANGES DEPENDING ON YOUR ANSWER. If the environment is in the threat model, the fix is small and local: extend the existing strip list, prove by regression that object reads and ref resolution cannot be redirected, and keep the one sanitization model rather than adding a second. If it is trusted, nothing changes in gitstore and I would suggest saying so in a comment beside hermeticGitEnvironment, so the next person who notices the asymmetry does not spend an evening on it as I did.\n\nONE THING THIS IS NOT. It is not the defect codex found in the remote-link lane. That one is inside internal/app, it is authorized, and it is fixed: five call sites there were redirectable and now strip the routing and pathspec families. This question is only about whether the same reasoning should reach the kernel boundary."}